A||||14||JohnnyK||I had to put up with some mocking today||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||09-01-2000||01:42 AM||||Well, I approached a stop light with two little punk kids in a Civic (I think it was. I am also a punk kid, but these were of the backwards hat persuation.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> They pulled up beside me and started laughing. I asked "You laughing at my car??" They said "Nope, I like the car, I'm laughing at you" and then drove off while saying something about a Ferrari. I think I heard something like "It's not a Ferrari!" Argh!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Now, I can admit the Japs make better cars than the americans, but this is starting to go too far.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> (I know a lot of people are going to argue that Americans make better cars, but lets face it, in terms of quality, the Japanese are better  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I'm going to have to start taking a rice-burning attitude.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||09-01-2000||02:53 AM||||well, part biased (being an american and all) but I have to rate american cars above japanesse cars. Take a mint '88 gt fiero, and rate it side by side w/ a mint '88 MR2, the '88 is a better car, and still holds a higher resale value. out side of fieros, how many early 70's japanesse cars are still out on the road? And sure, &gt;some&lt; of those rice burners are fast (don't be deceived by the stickers and farting noise) but even a japanesse engine can only take those super high v-tec RPMs for so long. Sure, a stock 2000 acord will beat an old fiero, but 12-16 years from now, will people be collecting acords?  not saying that american cars are tops tho... gotta love the Germans  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Porche, BMWs, and all that, and wasn't Delorian originally in Germany? maybe it's the beer...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||09-01-2000||11:59 AM||||The Delorean is/was all American.  It was the brainchild of the infamous John Z. Delorean (formerly of GM).  If it hadn't been for the coke and the IRS, it might still be in production.||||reg||1
